@issue-280 @bug @steering @verification
Feature: Distinguish zero steering validations from missing results
  As a developer using deterministic nmg-sdlc verification
  I want result coverage to reflect exact manifest declarations
  So that projects with no configured validations can proceed while missing configured evidence fails closed

  Scenario: Zero declared validations is a complete result set
    Given a schema-valid steering manifest declares zero validations
    When deterministic steering verification runs
    Then the artifact reports zero declared validations
    And the artifact records zero results
    And declaration/result coverage is complete
    And the ceiling is null

  Scenario: A missing declared result is incomplete
    Given the steering manifest declares a required validation
    And no result exists for that validation id
    When declaration/result coverage is evaluated
    Then the missing id is reported
    And coverage is incomplete
    And the verification ceiling is "Incomplete"

  Scenario Outline: Invalid result membership is incomplete
    Given the steering manifest declares known validation ids
    And the result set contains <condition>
    When declaration/result coverage is evaluated
    Then the offending ids are reported deterministically
    And coverage is incomplete
    And the verification ceiling is "Incomplete"

    Examples:
      | condition                    |
      | a duplicate validation id    |
      | an undeclared validation id  |

  Scenario: Complete declarations preserve required-result semantics
    Given every declared validation has exactly one result
    And declaration/result coverage is complete
    When the ordinary verification ceiling is computed
    Then applicable required validations retain their existing pass and failure rules
    And optional or non-applicable results do not weaken required checks

  Scenario: Consumers distinguish empty from missing
    Given the runner emits a machine-readable coverage summary
    When verify-code reads an artifact with zero declarations and zero results
    Then it does not infer "steering_results_missing" from the empty result array
    And generated and documented surfaces state the same rule
